@@s001 k-nearest neighbour: the exercise
@@s002 due:class A,dato:class B,ora:point to classify and its neighbours
@@s003 The 20 training points (10 of class A, 10 of class B) and the five points to classify, Q1…Q5. The distance is the Euclidean one. For each Q we look for the closest points and let the majority vote.
@@s004 <b>Q1 = (3, 3)</b>. Neighbours in order: A1 at distance 1, A2 at √2, B1 at 2, B2 at √5, A5 at √8. With k = 2, 3, 5 the votes are 2–0, 2–1, 3–2: always <b>A</b>. A stable answer.
@@s005 <b>Q2 = (2, 4)</b>. The five closest are all A (distances 1, 2, √5, √5, √8). A3 and A7 are tied, but both are A: nothing changes. Always <b>A</b>.
@@s006 <b>Q3 = (5, 2)</b>. Neighbours: B1 at 1, B3 and B8 at √2, B2 and B5 at 2: all B. Always <b>B</b>.
@@s007 <b>Q4 = (4, 5)</b> lies between the two groups. The closest is A2 (distance 1). Then A1, B2 and B7 are <b>all three at √2</b>, and A10 and B4 at 2. With k = 2 the second place goes to one of the three: A1 gives 2–0, B2 or B7 give 1–1. With k = 3 and k = 5 the result depends on which of the tied points you pick: A or B.
@@s008 <b>Q5 = (4, 2)</b>. First B1 (√2), second A2 (2), then <b>four points at √5</b>: A1, B2, B3, B8. k = 1: B. k = 2: B1 and A2, one each, a tied vote. k = 3: the third is one of the four tied points, so A or B. k = 5: B in every case (at most A2 and A1 against three B).
@@s009 Q1, Q2, Q3 lie inside a group: the answer does not depend on k. Q4 and Q5 lie between the two groups, close to the boundary: there k and the ties make the difference.
@@s010 due:class A,dato:class B,ora:same x₁ and x₂
@@s011 The point to classify is the origin, Q = (0, 0, 0, 0). With Q = 0 the squared distance of a point is the sum of the squares of its coordinates: A3 = (2, 2, 1, 0) gives 4 + 4 + 1 = 9. The points are put in order of distance; we compare the squares, because the root is increasing and the order does not change.
@@s012 <b>k = 2</b>: the two closest are A1 and A2. Two A: class <b>A</b>.
@@s013 <b>k = 3</b>: A1, A2 and B1. Votes 2–1: class <b>A</b>.
@@s014 <b>k = 5</b>: A1, A2, B1, B2, B3. Votes 2–3: class <b>B</b>. The answer changed because after B1 two more B entered.
@@s015 A3 = (2, 2, 1, 0) and B3 = (2, 2, 0, 0) have equal x₁ and x₂: in the plane they would be the <b>same point</b>, both at √8 from Q, tied for fifth place (with A3 A would win, with B3 B would win). What separates them is x₃: it moves A3 from √8 ≈ 2.83 to 3 and lets B3 in. The fourth coordinate is 0 for everyone and does not count.
@@s016 due:class A,dato:class B,spento:least-squares line,ora:boundary with k = 1,ok:boundary with k = 5,no:wrong point
@@s017 The same 20 points. Which boundary separates A from B?
@@s018 <b>Linear classifier</b> (least squares on the labels ±1): w = [−1.074, 0.359, −0.075], that is the line x₁ = 2.993 + 0.2085·x₂. A single line for the whole plane. It gets one point wrong, A2 = (4, 4): 19 out of 20, accuracy 0.95.
@@s019 <b>k = 1</b>: every point of the plane takes the class of the closest training point. The boundary is one step after another and bends around single points (a bulge towards B around A2). 20 out of 20 on the training: memory never makes a mistake.
@@s020 <b>k = 5</b>: the vote of five neighbours smooths the boundary and the bulge around A2 disappears: A2 is now classified B (19 out of 20 on the training). The boundary looks more like the line.
@@s021 k-nearest neighbour: the exercise
@@s022 Exercise of week 10 <i>K-Nearest-Neighbour Classifier</i> (Tasks 1–4) and lecture 9. A new point takes the class of the majority of its k neighbours. Here we calculate by hand, with the data of the notebook, the ties that the eye misses, the effect of k, the effect of the dimensions and the comparison with the linear classifier.
@@s023 The k-nearest neighbour in four steps
@@s024 How a point q is classified
@@s025 There is no training and there are no weights: the model is the training set itself (<i>memory-based</i>). x is the vector of features of a point, y its class, N how many points there are.
@@s026 q is the new point, xᵢ the point i of the training set; the second index says which feature we mean. It is the Euclidean distance, that is the norm of q − xᵢ (page <a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">Norms and distances</a>). It is computed for all N points.
@@s027 sort the distances and keep the <mark class="ora">k</mark> closest points
@@s028 k is chosen by whoever uses the method: it is a <b>hyperparameter</b>, not learned from the data.
@@s029 class of q = the most frequent among the k neighbours: <mark class="ok">majority vote</mark>
@@s030 With two classes and odd k a tied vote is impossible. With even k it can happen.
@@s031 Tasks 1–2: the 20 points and the five questions
@@s032 The notebook gives 10 points of class A, 10 of class B and five points to classify. Task 1 only asks to look at them; Task 2 asks for the class of each Q with k = 2, 3, 5 "by eye". Here we calculate the distances, and see where the eye is not enough.
@@s033 Class A
@@s034 Class B
@@s035 The names A1…A10 and B1…B10 are the ones the notebook writes on the plot, in the order in which they appear in the lists <code>class_A</code> and <code>class_B</code>. The five questions are Q1 = (3, 3), Q2 = (2, 4), Q3 = (5, 2), Q4 = (4, 5), Q5 = (4, 2).
@@s036 The five questions, one at a time
@@s037 Point
@@s038 A, or a tied vote 1–1
@@s039 tied vote 1–1
@@s040 The votes are written as "how many A – how many B". Where the cell says "A / B" the result is A or B, depending on how the tie explained below is resolved.
@@s041 The ties: what the eye misses
@@s042 With integer coordinates distances repeat: from Q4 three points are all at √2. If the k-th place is contested by several points at the same distance, <b>the method alone does not decide</b>: a rule is needed. The most common: keep all the tied points (then there are more than k neighbours), choose by the order of the data, or weight the votes by distance. In the exercise the honest thing is to say that there is a tie and that the answer depends on how it is resolved.
@@s043 With <code>KNeighborsClassifier</code> of scikit-learn 1.9.0 the output is: for Q4, A with k = 2, 3, 5; for Q5, A with k = 2 and 3, B with k = 5. It picks among the tied points itself and, on a tied vote, gave A: this is an implementation detail, not a rule of the method.
@@s044 Safe points and points on the boundary
@@s045 Q1, Q2 and Q3 lie inside a group: the neighbours are almost all of the same class and changing k does not change the answer. Q4 and Q5 lie between the two groups, close to the <b>decision boundary</b>: there any change of k, of distance or of tie can flip the class.
@@s046 How the answer changes with k (Task 2)
@@s047 The question of the notebook: what happens when k is very small or very large, and how does it relate to overfitting and underfitting?
@@s048 <b>Small k</b> (at the limit 1): a single point decides. A point out of place, because of noise or measurement error, changes the answer all around it. The boundary is jagged and follows the noise: <b>overfitting</b>.
@@s049 <b>Large k</b>: every answer is an average over many points. The boundary smooths out; real details are lost. At the limit k = N all the points vote for every Q: here 10–10, a single class everywhere, 0.5 accuracy: <b>underfitting</b>.
@@s050 <b>In between</b> there is the right k, and it is chosen with a validation set (<a href="w07-validazione.html">Validation</a>), because k is a hyperparameter.
@@s051 training points got wrong
@@s052 accuracy on the training
@@s053 none
@@s054 none
@@s055 all the B
@@s056 <code>KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=k).fit(X, y).predict(X)</code>. In the training set every point is also a neighbour of itself (distance 0): that is why k = 1 always gives 1.0. An accuracy of 1.0 on the training says nothing about generalisation.
@@s057 knn(q, k): the votes of the k neighbours (verified with the data of the notebook)
@@s058 All the points in a 20 × 2 matrix and, in the same order, their classes: 0 for A, 1 for B. Both are needed because the vote reads the class of a point from its index.
@@s059 <code>X - q</code> subtracts q from every row of X (broadcasting): these are the vectors from q to every point. <code>norm</code> with <code>axis=1</code> computes their length row by row: 20 distances.
@@s060 <code>argsort</code> gives the indices of the points from the closest to the farthest; <code>[:k]</code> keeps the first k. With <code>kind="stable"</code> at equal distance the order of the data stays, that is the A before the B: this is the tie rule of this code.
@@s061 <code>y[vicini]</code> are the classes of the k neighbours; <code>np.bincount</code> counts them. <code>minlength=2</code> guarantees two slots even if no neighbour is B. Result for Q4 with k = 3: <code>[2 1]</code>, two A and one B.
@@s062 With this rule (A before B) the code gives, for k = 2, 3, 5: Q1 [2 0], [2 1], [3 2]; Q2 [2 0], [3 0], [5 0]; Q3 [0 2], [0 3], [0 5]; Q4 [2 0], [2 1], [3 2]; Q5 [1 1], [2 1], [2 3]. The "A / B" cells of the table are those where another rule would give a different result.
@@s063 Task 3: more dimensions
@@s064 The notebook gives a second set, in four dimensions, with a single point to classify, Q = [0, 0, 0, 0]. The distance is computed as before, with four terms under the root.
@@s065 Class A
@@s066 Class B
@@s067 From the closest to the farthest, in four dimensions
@@s068 In this example no distance repeats, so there is no tie.
@@s069 <b>Why the dimension matters</b>: A3 and B3 have the same x₁ and x₂ and differ only in x₃. In two dimensions they would be the same point and the fifth place would be a tie (A or B). The third coordinate separates them and decides the fifth place: one more feature changes the answer, even if it says nothing useful about the class.
@@s070 More features, less informative distances
@@s071 Every extra coordinate adds a term to the distance and can move the neighbours. A useless feature (or one on a much larger scale than the others) brings noise into the distance. With many dimensions, moreover, distances become almost all equal and the "closest" is not much closer than the others: see the curse of dimensionality in <a href="w12-pca.html">PCA</a>.
@@s072 Task 4: linear classifier versus neighbours
@@s073 The question of the notebook: how do the decision boundaries differ, and how do they differ from the point of view of computation? On the same 20 points:
@@s074 A line, or the neighbours
@@s075 Linear classifier
@@s076 k-nearest neighbour
@@s077 What it learns
@@s078 the weights w: d + 1 numbers (d features)
@@s079 nothing: it keeps the whole training set, N × d numbers
@@s080 Boundary
@@s081 just one, a hyperplane (here a line)
@@s082 made of pieces, follows the data; for small k it bends around single points
@@s083 Training
@@s084 yes: least squares or gradient descent, cost paid once
@@s085 none (it is memorised)
@@s086 Prediction
@@s087 one inner product: cost proportional to d
@@s088 distances from all N points: cost proportional to N · d, plus choosing the k
@@s089 Scale of the features
@@s090 the weight w compensates for a different scale
@@s091 matters a lot: a feature on a large scale dominates the distance
@@s092 Shape of the data
@@s093 good if the classes are separable by a hyperplane
@@s094 good also with boundaries of any shape, if there is enough data
@@s095 On the 20 points
@@s096 19 out of 20 (0.95), gets A2 wrong
@@s097 k = 1: 20 out of 20; k = 5: 19 out of 20
@@s098 The line is the least-squares one on the labels ±1, as in the page <a href="w10-classificatore-lineare.html">Linear classifier and logistic regression</a>. The boundaries of k = 1 and k = 5 are computed with scikit-learn on a grid of 32 × 32 cells.
@@s099 At the exam, in English
@@s100 "k-nearest neighbour <b>stores the training set</b> and classifies a new point by a <b>majority vote among its k closest training points</b>, using a distance such as the Euclidean one. A small k gives a jagged boundary and overfits; a large k smooths it and can underfit; I choose k on a validation set."
